HBPLv1 printer status: the STS field of the IEEE 1284 device ID
================================================================

hbpl1status(1) reads the status of HBPLv1 printers from their IEEE 1284
device ID.  This file documents what is known about that data.

Observed on a Dell 1250c (USB 413c:5404) in October 2026.  The Dell 1250c
is a rebadged Fuji Xerox DocuPrint CP105b / Xerox Phaser 6000, so the same
layout is likely, but NOT confirmed, on those printers.  Reports from other
HBPLv1 printers (Dell C1660w/C1760nw, Epson AcuLaser C1700, Phaser 6010,
CP105b) are welcome: run "hbpl1status /dev/usb/lp0" and compare.

The device ID
-------------

    MFG:Dell;CMD:PJL,RASTER,DOWNLOAD,HBPL;MDL:1250c Color Printer;
    DES:Dell 1250c Color Printer;CLS:PRINTER;STS:AAARBQD/AP8AAAAAAAIAAWQj...;

The printer rebuilds the device ID on every request (USB GET_DEVICE_ID class
request), so STS is live.  It can be read:

  - with the usblp ioctl LPIOC_GET_DEVICE_ID on /dev/usb/lpN (Linux);
  - from a CUPS filter, with cupsSideChannelDoRequest(CUPS_SC_CMD_GET_DEVICE_ID);
    the libusb usb backend answers it with a fresh control transfer;
  - from /usr/lib/cups/backend/usb (macOS) when no job is running.

The Linux sysfs copy (/sys/class/usbmisc/lp0/device/ieee1284_id) is read
once when the device is probed and is NOT live.

STS is base64 wrapped like MIME: on Linux it contains "\r\n" after 76
characters, and macOS shows it with spaces.  Remove all white space before
decoding.  It decodes to 58 bytes.

Reading the device ID never sends data to the printer, so it is safe while
the printer is idle or busy.  Do not use PJL to query these printers:
"@PJL INFO ID" and "@PJL INFO STATUS" answer, but "@PJL INFO CONFIG" put the
printer into error 016-799 for about 90 seconds, without a reply (no page
was printed).  hbpl1status never writes to the printer.

Layout
------

Reference, idle, all cartridges in (Black at 80 %):

    00 00 11 05 00 ff 00 ff 00 00 00 00 00 02 00 01
    64 23 64 23 64 23 50 23 00 00 00 00 64 03 40 00
    00 ... 00 ff ff

  Offset  Meaning                                              Status
  ------  ---------------------------------------------------  ---------
  0-1     00 00                                                unknown
  2       engine state, see below                              partly
  3       sub-state, see below                                 partly
  4-5     error code, first part, big endian (00 ff: none)     confirmed
  6-7     error code, second part, big endian (00 ff: none)    confirmed
  8-11    00 00 00 00                                          unknown
  12      01 while a job is in the printer (from data arrival  observed
          until the page is out, and during a paper-out stop)
  13-14   02 00                                                unknown
  15      01 normally, 00 while a cover was open               observed
  16, 17  Yellow level (percent), flag                         confirmed
  18, 19  Magenta level, flag                                  confirmed
  20, 21  Cyan level, flag                                     confirmed
  22, 23  Black level, flag                                    confirmed
  24-27   00 00 00 00                                          unknown
  28-31   64 03 40 00, unchanged so far                        unknown
  32-55   zero
  56-57   ff ff

Toner level and flag: the level is a percentage (0x64 = 100).  The flag is
0x23 when the cartridge is in place.  With a cartridge removed, its level
reads 0x7f and its flag 0x20 (confirmed for each of the four colours).
How a low or empty cartridge is flagged has not been observed yet;
hbpl1status reports "low" below 15 % and "empty" at 0 %.

Error codes are the codes the printer shows as "MMM-NNN": bytes 4-5 are MMM
and bytes 6-7 NNN.  Observed:

  093-970  Yellow toner cartridge missing    (removed it: confirmed)
  093-971  Magenta toner cartridge missing   (confirmed)
  093-972  Cyan toner cartridge missing      (confirmed)
  093-973  Black toner cartridge missing     (confirmed)
  024-969  paper empty                       (confirmed: no paper, then a job)
  077-300  front cover open                  (confirmed)
  016-799  job error                         (after "@PJL INFO CONFIG")

Paper empty is only reported once the printer tries to feed a sheet: an
idle printer with an empty tray still reports no error.  Opening the toner
access door (without removing a cartridge) changes nothing in STS.

Engine state (bytes 2 and 3), as observed:

  16 01   ready (Continue LED green); held for about 5 minutes after power
          on, after a job or after an error clears
  11 05   power saver, entered about 5 minutes after the last activity
  11 04   power saver, second stage, exactly 6 minutes after 11 05
          (the fan stops and the Continue LED turns orange during one of
          these two; which one is not settled)
  11 07   for a few seconds, when an error clears (likely)
  12 00   printing (job data arriving, page in progress)
  10 04   stopped: paper empty (with 024-969)
  0d 0N   stopped: toner cartridge N missing (1=Y, 2=M, 3=C, 4=K)
  0b 03   stopped: job error 016-799
  0f 00   stopped: front cover open (077-300)

Reading the device ID does not wake the printer from power saver (it was
read every 2 seconds while the printer went to 11 05 and 11 04).

Bytes 28-31 (64 03 40 00) are likely another consumable or maintenance
counter, by analogy with the toner bytes (0x64 = 100 %, then a flag), but
they did not change during any test so far.

CUPS
----

hbpl1status -c turns this into:

    ATTR: marker-colors=#000000,#00FFFF,#FF00FF,#FFFF00
    ATTR: marker-names="Black Toner","Cyan Toner","Magenta Toner","Yellow Toner"
    ATTR: marker-types=toner,toner,toner,toner
    ATTR: marker-low-levels=15,15,15,15
    ATTR: marker-high-levels=100,100,100,100
    ATTR: marker-levels=80,100,100,100
    STATE: -toner-low-warning
    STATE: -toner-empty-error
    STATE: -marker-supply-missing-error
    STATE: -media-empty-error
    STATE: -media-needed
    STATE: -cover-open-error

A missing cartridge gets level -1 and marker-supply-missing-error.
If the toner bytes don't look like the layout above (level over 100 other
than 0x7f, or a flag outside 0x20-0x3f), nothing is reported.

marker-names is only sent when the queue doesn't already have the same
names and a printer-supply-description: CUPS 2.4 (scheduler/printers.c,
cupsdSetPrinterAttr) looks up the previous printer-supply-description with
IPP_TAG_TEXT, but the copy it made keeps IPP_TAG_NAME, so every update adds
a duplicate attribute (ipptool then fails with 'Duplicate
"printer-supply-description" attribute').

The levels reach CUPS in two ways:

  - foo2hbpl1-wrapper runs "hbpl1status -s -c" at the start of every job.
    The side channel (fd 4) survives foomatic-rip.  The levels are those
    before the job.
  - The CUPS filter command2foo2hbpl1 (a link to hbpl1status) handles the
    ReportLevels command.  modify-ppd adds it to the PPD:

        *cupsFilter:	"application/vnd.cups-command 100 command2foo2hbpl1"
        *cupsCommands:	"ReportLevels"

    and this updates the levels at any time, without printing (the filter
    writes nothing, the usb backend logs "Sent 0 bytes"):

        printf '#CUPS-COMMAND\nReportLevels\n' | lp -d <queue>
